Z||000015||ds21||09-29-2001||11:23 PM||||Artherd; looks like a nice set-up, using focals for home too I see. Well for my Fiero stereo I'm going to be using my old 550 celeron, a LCD module, was thinking about voice activation with an instant mute button, but decided it wouldn't be reliable, maybe touch-screen but don't know how it would stand-up to the WI winters, maybe Joe Torma can let me know how his does? for now just going to take apart a game pad and use it. For the software, Winamp but designed my own interface (not using skins)for more efficiency using a small 5.6inch LCD. Only using computer, no head unit or cd changer. Rip cd on home computer than have Fiero computer check home computer for any new mp3's though my wireless network cards, will also download whether and movies times ...<P>Sootah,<BR>Building your own speakers takes experience to get right,I started out with a friend, going to radioshack got about 6 or 7 drivers a pre-built X-over, and were going to build just one speakers "more is better right?" wrong! ended up making 2 little bookshelf speakers. Then got into car audio and got really good at that, a couple years later my sister who plays guitar and sings wanted some stage monitors and that got me going on the never ending quest for better sound! 1.Get a book, RadioShack has a good little one to start with. 2.Get a computer program, free on the internet. 3.Keep it simple to start, maybe use some pre-built X-overs than build your own later, X-overs can make or break a speaker. Also keep in mind that a good sub/bass driver for the car probably won't sound good for the home. I can make good sounding subs for the car in my sleep (well most of the time), but for home...<P>Good place for mailorder drivers and X-over parts <A HREF="http://www.madisound.com" TARGET=_blank>www.madisound.com</A>  I live in the same city so it's nice for me, also <A HREF="http://www.partsexpress.com" TARGET=_blank>www.partsexpress.com</A> <p>[This message has been edited by ds21 (edited 09-29-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000016||AutoXdbdragSE||09-30-2001||12:39 AM||||I have two rockford fosgate HE2 12's mounted behind the seats for DBdrag, in 3 cubic feet ported boxes. each one running off a 250A2<P>for sq i have fanatic p 6.5's in the doors, with 8" rockford fosgate punch subs for midbass.. during sq i run one 12 in the passenger futwell to fill out the bass.. the one 12 runs off a 250aII and the 6.5's and the 8's run of a 250aII in tri mode with custom built crossover network....<P>i got a question for you all.. i know i'm new to this forum, but i've owned my fiero for 6 years, had my liscence for 2, and been playin with car audio for 5 years, and i have built a stock style subwoofer enclosure out of fiberglass that instead of the 6" sub houses a very standard 8" sub..  it is approxamatly .75 cubic ft, and could be built ported or not, depending on sub specs.. i am wondering if i where to begin manufactuing these, in a small run, as it is a hobby, and offer them soley based on shipping, supplies, and a little labor cost, would people be interested.  i'm thinking cost would be around $50 since fiberglass isn't teribly expensive, and with the form it wouldn't be terribly difficult to build.. let me know if this would generate significant interest..  i would just like to spread my love of fieros and fiero stereo out.. i mean this is my hobby, so i'm just looking to have fun.. thanks, and let me know if you are interested||||reg||1
Z||000017||Philphine||09-30-2001||01:18 AM||||i'd like to see/hear more about it. sounds like it might be good in an indy if it became a driver. i'm trying not to change it much.<P>i'd also like to know how you got 12's behind the seats and how much leg room you might have lost (how tall are you?). i may need to go big in my gt. ||||reg||1
Z||000018||Joe Torma||09-30-2001||01:25 PM||||AutoX...I'm sure you'll get a lot of orders for your sub boxes @ $50.  Really nice setup.<BR>I don't know how the touchscreen is going to like the cold...yet.  I'm sure LCD screens get a little slow when frozen though.<P>POST SOME PICS!!!<BR>If you haven't figured it out yet...the PIP link at the bottom of the page will tell you how. ||||reg||1
Z||000019||Capt Fiero||09-30-2001||01:34 PM||||I have competed, not as a pro, and it was real fun, but I got so into AMP's and Stereo's that it eventually started to take over the interior of my car with speakers and crossovers, and amps, oh my.  I managed a 132db rating at the last sound-off I did. It was a loudness test not a clarity test.  I have since cut my sound system back, I know run 2 -- 5 1/4 dash speakers in custom plates, with 2 -- 2" dome tweats pointed directly at the passanger and driver, as well as the 4 stock seat speakers.  I do still have an ADS competion 8" Sub in a box that I sometimes toss back under the passanger side dash and hook it up to my existing amp for the front 5 1/4's but I am really into racing now, and really dont want to have 100+ pounds of stereo in my car when I hit the track.<P>------------------<BR>David<BR>85GT<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://photos.onebigvillage.com/pubimage.asp?id_=32415"> ||||reg||1
Z||000020||Joe Torma||09-30-2001||03:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Capt Fiero:<BR><B>... and really dont want to have 100+ pounds of stereo in my car when I hit the track.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>*sigh*  I know what you mean.  My system is going to weigh about 200lbs.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">   The computer weighs less than the spare tire & jack, and the 3400 weighs 75-100lbs(removed lots of cr@p) less than the 2.8.  So my car should be about 100lbs over stock.<P>||||reg||1
